Cypress Test Automation Project

This project contains automated Frontend and API tests using Cypress, Cucumber, and Allure Report.

The project follows a structured approach for automated testing, separating API services, page objects, step definitions, feature files, and test scenarios.

It also includes CI/CD pipelines for GitHub Actions and GitLab CI, allowing automated test execution, Allure report generation, and artifact uploads.

Prerequisites

Before running the project, make sure you have:

Node.js

npm

Git

Verify the installations:

node --version
npm --version
git --version

Clone the Repository

git clone <REPOSITORY_URL>
cd project-folder-name

Example:

git clone git@github.com:LinoVieiraSantos/Cypress-E2E-Automation.git
cd Cypress-E2E-Automation

Cypress Version

This project requires Cypress 15.9.0.

The project uses this Cypress version to maintain compatibility with the current testing and reporting dependencies, including:

@badeball/cypress-cucumber-preprocessor

allure-cypress

cypress-on-fix

The recommended Cypress version is defined in package.json:

"cypress": "15.9.0"

After cloning the repository, install the exact project dependencies with:

npm install

You can verify the installed Cypress version with:

npx cypress --version

Expected version:

Cypress package version: 15.9.0

Install Dependencies

Install all project dependencies:

npm install

Run Cypress

Open Cypress in Interactive Mode

npm run test:open

Or:

npx cypress open

Run the Complete Test Suite

npm test

Or:

npx cypress run

Run Tests in Headed Mode

npm run test:headed

Run a Specific Feature

npx cypress run --spec "cypress/e2e/path/to/feature.feature"

Examples:

npx cypress run --spec "cypress/e2e/api/cart/createCart.feature"

npx cypress run --spec "cypress/e2e/api/cart/cancelPurchase.feature"

npx cypress run --spec "cypress/e2e/frontend/login.feature"

Allure Report

Allure generates detailed and interactive reports for both Frontend and API test executions.

Run Tests and Generate Results

npm test

Allure result files are generated in:

allure-results

Verify the generated files:

ls -la allure-results

Generate the Allure Report

npm run allure:generate

Equivalent command:

npx allure generate allure-results --clean -o allure-report

The generated report is located in:

allure-report

Open the Allure Report

npm run allure:open

Equivalent command:

npx allure open allure-report

Complete Execution Flow

Run Cypress Tests
        ↓
Generate Allure Results
        ↓
Generate Allure HTML Report
        ↓
Open Interactive Report

Commands:

npm test
npm run allure:generate
npm run allure:open

CI/CD

The project includes automated CI/CD pipelines for GitHub Actions and GitLab CI.

The pipelines can:

Run automated tests automatically

Execute the Cypress test suite

Generate Allure test results

Generate the Allure HTML report

Upload results as artifacts

Upload the Allure report as an artifact

GitHub Actions

The GitHub Actions workflow is located at:

.github/workflows/cypress-tests.yml

Typical workflow:

Code Push
    ↓
GitHub Actions Starts
    ↓
Install Dependencies
    ↓
Run Cypress Tests
    ↓
Generate Allure Results
    ↓
Generate Allure Report
    ↓
Upload Artifacts

GitLab CI

The GitLab pipeline configuration is located at:

.gitlab-ci.yml

Typical pipeline:

Code Push
    ↓
GitLab Pipeline Starts
    ↓
Install Dependencies
    ↓
Run Cypress Tests
    ↓
Generate Allure Results
    ↓
Generate Allure Report
    ↓
Upload Artifacts

CI/CD Artifacts

The pipeline can preserve:

allure-results

and:

allure-report

These artifacts can be downloaded or inspected from the CI/CD platform after pipeline execution.

CI/CD Status Badge

A GitHub Actions status badge can be added to the top of the README:

![Cypress Tests](https://github.com/<USERNAME>/<REPOSITORY>/actions/workflows/cypress-tests.yml/badge.svg)

Replace <USERNAME> and <REPOSITORY> with the repository information.

GitLab pipeline badges can also be configured directly from the GitLab project settings.

Frontend Test Scenario Example

Scenario: Successful Login with a Registered User

Feature: User Login

Scenario: Successful login with registered user

  Given I visit the login page

  When I login with the registered user

  Then I should be redirected to the home page

Test Flow

Create a registered user

        ↓

User accesses the application

        ↓

User navigates to the Login page

        ↓

User enters valid credentials

        ↓

User clicks the Login button

        ↓

The system authenticates the user

        ↓

The user is redirected to the home page

API Test Scenario Example

Scenario: Successfully Create a Shopping Cart

Feature: Shopping Cart

Scenario: Successfully create a shopping cart

  Given an authenticated user exists

  And an available product exists

  When I create a shopping cart with the product

  Then the cart should be created successfully

Test Flow

Create a new user
        ↓
Validate HTTP status 201
        ↓
Authenticate the user
        ↓
Receive the authentication token
        ↓
Create an available product
        ↓
Validate HTTP status 201
        ↓
Retrieve the product ID
        ↓
Create a shopping cart containing the product
        ↓
Send the authentication token
        ↓
Validate HTTP status 201
        ↓
Validate the cart ID and success message

Purchase Cancellation and Stock Restoration Example

Scenario: Restore Product Stock When Cancelling a Purchase

Scenario: Should restore product stock when cancelling a purchase

  Given an authenticated user with an existing cart

  When I complete the purchase

  And I cancel the purchase

  Then the product stock should be restored

Test Flow

Create a new user
        ↓
Authenticate the user
        ↓
Retrieve the authentication token
        ↓
Create a product with available stock
        ↓
Create a shopping cart
        ↓
Complete the purchase
        ↓
The product stock is updated
        ↓
Cancel the purchase
        ↓
The product stock is restored
        ↓
Validate the original product quantity

